#p(3)[
  As with any machine learning task, preparing the data is crucial to allow the model to learn anything. Typically, inputs will need to be normalized or mapped to a specific range of values in order to prevent gradients from exploding or vanishing during SGD and optimization. This is the case in Instant-NGP. Below is a table containing information on how the data was normalized:
]

#p(1)[

  #table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: horizon,
    table.header([*Input*], [Input Range], [Normalized Range], [*Output*], [Input Range], [Normalized Range]),
    [
      _Ray Position_ $(x, y, z)$
    ],
    [
      Maximum of scene bounds
    ],
    [
      $[-1,1]$
    ],
    [
      _RGB Radiance_ $L_e (r, g, b)$
    ],
    [
      $[0, "Max float value"]$
    ],
    [
      #text(size: 8pt)[
        $[0,ln("Max" L_e + 1)]$
      ]
    ],

    [
      _Ray Direction_ $(x, y, z)$
    ],
    [
      [-1, 1]
    ],
    [
      [0, 1]
    ],
    [
      _RGB Transmittance_ $T (r, g, b)$
    ],
    [
      $[0, 1]$
    ],
    [
      $[0, 1]$
    ],

    [
      `tMax`
    ],
    [
      $[0,$"Max Scene bounds"$]$
    ],
    [
      $[0, 1]$
    ],
  )
]


#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Ray Position
]

#p(4)[
  In Instant-NGP, similar to the other modes such as NeRF and Volume, require ray positions to be normalized to be between $[-1, 1]$ and in fact in `nerf_loader.cu` to be compatible with the multi-resolution hash grid /*!TODO: Find the reason for this*/, it performs this normalization by dividing by the scene AABB. Therefore, during loading, I also need to note down the bounds of the scene in order to normalize. Below is a table of
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Ray Direction
]

#p(4)[
  Similarly for Ray directions in other Instant-NGP modes, they require to be normalized. Though, they need to be normalized to be between $[0, 1]$. The reason for this is because in Instant-NGP, directions are typically not encoded in the hashgrid, but instead in *Spherical Harmonics*, /*!TODO: Look up and write a bit about spherical harmonics*/ which is a different encoding but does well for directions.
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  `tMax`
]

#p(4)[
  tMax is an auxilliary input, but to prevent the final MLP from being overly reliant on this input and having it dominated, it requires normalization as well, which can easily be done by finding the max value of `tMax` within the dataset and dividing all `tMax` values by this. However, some care needs to take place as in PBRT, tMax can actually be arbitrarily large for cases of "escaped rays". Care needs to be selected to ensure that the max `tMax` value doesn't clamp rays that are still traveling within the medium itself. In this case, I performed clamping of a `tMax` value of $50$ which is justified in the Data Visualization section.

  /*!TODO: Ensure I add a note explaining why I clamped it to just 50 (since the 160 is likely escaped rays and we don't want the network to learn "escaped rays"*/
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Radiance $L_e$
]

#p(4)[
  The outputs for the model need to be carefully normalized in order for the model loss values to meaningfully update the model weights through SGD. However, because of the fact that radiance values themselves can be arbitrarily large due to radiance being able to represent High Dynamic Range (HDR), if there are any fireflies in the training data due to the Monte Carlo Estimation, /*!TODO: Make sure to add Monte Carlo estimation in the theoretical background*/ linear normalization $L/L_"max"$ preserves extreme sparsity of the emission distribution and thus empty space with emission $0$ and some less emitting parts of the explosion which may have a radiance of $1$ would be overshadowed by the core of the explosion or part with extreme radiance emission (say $100$) so after the linear scaling the less emitting smoke would have a value of $10^(-3)$ and the core would be $1.0$. Thus the network will overfit to those higher peaks and ignore the lower-intensity radiances.

  Instead, I employed logarithmic scaling by transforming radiance values into $y=ln(L + 1)$ to compress that high dynamic range. This ensures the loss function minimizes relative error instead of absolute. This ensures that the network has enough capacity to learn both high and low emission as opposed to higher emission dominating. It also keeps the output range to be in a similar magnitude as Transmittance to ensure it doesn't dominate (Adam optimizer deals well with losses with similar magnitude). The $+1$ term ensures stability for areas that don't have emission.
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Transmittance $T$
]

#p(4)[
  For transmittance, we take the approach to ensure that it is in the range $[0, 1]$ as it only makes sense if we consider it as a fraction of light that transmits through. However, due to the logging code and how I performed calculations to calculate the isolated transmittance within one ray sampling step in `media.cpp`
  ```cpp
  Float pr = T_maj[0] * mp.sigma_s[0];
  beta *= T_maj * mp.sigma_s / pr;
  .
  .
  .
  SampledSpectrum T_target = beta / beta_before;
  ```
  causes some $T$ values to have a higher transmittance value $1$. For example, if the path already had a $beta=0.5$ meaning the cumulative throughput before this bounce was only 0.5 and then the beta after was calculated to be 0.8 (due to random sampling and probability above) /*!TODO: Look in pbrt-v4 to confirm this*/ then the overall transmittance value is calculated to be $>1$. However, this may not only cause divergence in training, but also doesn't make physical sense. Therefore, I chose to clamp these values to 1 as this will still allow the network to learn what should phyically happen other than this noise due to random sampling.
]





